WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.490
ALOK JAIN: Hello and welcome
to What's New in Anthos.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.980
In this session, we will go
over exciting new additions

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.410
to Anthos, which will
make it even more easier

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.160
for you to manage and secure
your multi-cloud and hybrid

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.810
applications.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.720
We have been working hard
to add more features,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.470
expand reach, and simplify
the experiences to make Anthos

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.840
the best application
modernization platform.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.950
I'm Alok Jain.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.130
And I lead Product Management
for Anthos at Google.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.270
Joining us in this session
is my colleague Lisa Shen.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.660
LISA SHEN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:43.730
My name is Lisa Shen.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:47.960
And I am a Google
Cloud product manager.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.550
ALOK JAIN: Before
we dive deep, here's

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.010
a quick introduction for
those who are new to Anthos.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.800
Let's go back a few years.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.720
We created Kubernetes
to maximize

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.690
productivity of our own
developers at Google.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.660
And then we open sourced it to
help others achieve the same.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.340
To make Kubernetes
production-ready,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.950
we created Google
Kubernetes Engine--

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.110
or GKE, as we call it--

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.970
the best way to
consume Kubernetes

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.720
as a reliable, secure,
and fully-managed service.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.400
In 2019, we introduced Anthos,
a managed platform designed

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.710
to simplify the
management of Kubernetes

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.650
cluster or any public
or private cloud

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.150
by extending a GKE-like
experience along with our best

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.610
open source frameworks.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.430
Anthos extends Google
engineering practices

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.060
to your environment, so that you
can modernize your apps faster

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.330
and establish operational
consistency across them

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.190
from a single pane of glass.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.500
It helps you minimize
operational overhead

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.320
as you scale your infrastructure
and helps you reduce

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.870
the total cost of ownership.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.830
Containerization offers a common
application packaging format

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.080
across different
infrastructure types.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.580
And Kubernetes offers consistent
APIs and control mechanisms,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.400
and thus can be used for
application deployments

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.560
on your existing
virtual machines,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.030
or bare metal servers, or even
on other public clouds like AWS

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:12.900
and Azure--

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.280
besides Google Cloud.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.010
Anthos is made up of
a few core components.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.440
Infrastructure
management provides

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.930
a reliable and efficient
way to deploy and run

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.180
containers and manage them
across any environment.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.180
Next is application
service management--

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.100
it provides managed service mesh
to connect, manage, and secure

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.210
containers.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.310
We extend the
capabilities of Anthos

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.150
to run serverless
applications as well.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.050
It also has a wide selection of
production-related development

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.210
stacks and services.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.850
Operation management is
an integrated logging

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.400
and monitoring service for
your container, services,

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.080
and applications.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.110
Policy management and
security management

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.570
are central to manage
configuration and security

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.790
policies across your fleet.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.730
And it helps you reduce
your operational cost--

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.580
at the same time, enforce
security posture that's

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.710
right for your applications.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.080
With that, we are now
ready to jump right

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:16.400
in and talk about the new and
exciting additions to Anthos.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.220
Anthos MultiCloud allows
container orchestration

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.660
standardization across clouds
via a new managed service.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.730
With this new
service, we've also

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.610
added support for
Anthos and Azure.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.160
Both of these are in
preview with GA coming right

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.590
around the corner.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.790
So now you can deploy
Google Kubernetes clusters

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:42.110
with GCP, AWS, and Azure
directly from Google Cloud.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:46.130
You can control all cluster
lifecycle operations centrally.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.800
You can review system
and application logs

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.960
and monitor clusters
across all clouds

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.280
through a single pane of glass.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.380
Cluster lifecycle
operations can be controlled

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.820
via G. Cloud, API, or Terraform,
allowing for a completely

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.500
automated workflow.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.500
As a result, you get
consistent, unified,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.110
and secure cluster and
container management

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.580
on a cloud of your choice.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.940
In addition to centralized
logging and monitoring,

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.130
you benefit from
our connect gateway,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.500
which allows for Google Cloud
to be the central access

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.029
point for each cluster.

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:22.070
We'll talk more
about it in a minute.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.110
After you've deployed Kubernetes
fabric in your environment,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.190
the next step is to deploy
cluster-level configuration

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.160
policy.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.750
And finally, application specs--

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.540
choosing Anthos configuration
management service,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.060
you can send GKE
and non-GKE cluster

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.700
to a Git repo that
defines the guardrails

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.530
for your organization.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.220
The repo can include config
maps, network policies,

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.680
SRE agents, and so on.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.160
Policy configurations can
also be located in the repo

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.710
to enforce best practices
or custom rules set

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.400
by your organization.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.150
For example, you can
set policies to actively

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.570
block non-compliant
API requests or simply

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.490
to audit the configuration
of your clusters

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:06.640
and report violations,
such as applications

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.260
without proper labels.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.700
Configuration specs
can be applied

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:15.570
to all clusters or just a subset
using Cluster Selector Object.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.400
Any changes to the
config in the repo

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.790
will be observed
automatically by the cluster.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.320
And besides GKE, Anthos supports
CMC of confirmed Kubernetes

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.770
clusters like AKS or EKS,
giving you the option

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.190
to keep your application running
on your favorite Kubernetes

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.530
and still take advantage
of Anthos services.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.010
Next is Anthos on bare metal.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.440
It lets you deploy
Kubernetes clusters directly

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.410
on your own servers, giving
you the best performance

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.010
and flexibility options.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.950
You have direct control over
the application scale, security,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.460
network latency by running
containerized application

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:59.097
on GKE and benefit from Anthos
components running locally.

00:05:59.097 --> 00:06:00.680
We have made a number
of announcements

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.810
recently to Anthos
on bare metal,

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.560
such as providing Containerd
as a default runtime,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.810
adding registry
building that helps

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:13.620
you insulate from any service
outages over the network

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.480
and helps improve the security.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:19.480
It will also reduce
minimum system requirement

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:25.110
for running Anthos on bare metal
by over 50% for edge use cases.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:28.620
Now you will only need
one of the two PCPUs

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.300
and only four
gigabytes of memory

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.280
to run Anthos on bare metal.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.130
That means more
compute and more memory

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.640
available for your applications.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.880
It reduces the overall TCO.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.540
And you have many edge locations
with a small footprint.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.150
Anthos on bare metal brings
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:52.260
to optimized centers with broad
support for vSphere platforms,

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.990
including the latest
stable release 7.0.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.810
We have rolled out a number
of features on Anthos VMware

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.170
this year.

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:03.460
For example, with the Cluster
Backup and Restore feature,

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.010
you can now set up
admin plus 10 backups

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:10.030
to perform automatically before
and after cluster creation

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.890
or upgrade for user
and admin clusters.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.860
You can also perform
an on-demand backup

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.420
whenever you deem it necessary.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.530
Another feature is User
Cluster Autoscaling,

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.540
which resizes the
number of nodes

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.720
in a given node pool based
on demand of your workloads.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.750
You don't need to manually
add or remove nodes or even

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.840
overprovision your node pools
to guarantee availability--

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.440
and thereby adding to the cost.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.160
Instead, you can specify
a minimum and maximum size

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.330
for the node pole and the rest
is taken care automatically.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.870
Last year, we announced support
for Windows server containers

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.740
on GCP, which lets you take
advantage of containers

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.260
without putting in
applications to darknet code

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.011
or rewriting them.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.250
We have now enabled support
for Windows server container

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.520
and Anthos for VMware embedded
in your on-prem environment.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.640
By running Windows and Linux
workloads side by side,

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.790
you get operational
consistency and efficiency.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.190
There is no need to have
multiple teams specializing

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.170
in different
tooling or platforms

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.490
to manage different workloads.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.180
Mixed workloads can be
configured on a single cluster

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.780
as well, giving you
even more flexibility.

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.720
The ability to manage policies
from a central control plane

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.330
further simplifies the
management experience,

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:31.180
while bin-packing multiple
Windows applications

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.490
drives better
resource utilization,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:36.175
leading to infrastructure
and license cost savings.

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:41.380
We have recently made Anthos
Connect Gateway generally

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.690
available for our customers.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.750
It provides a single
Google-hosted front load,

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.510
allowing for customers to
use their existing tooling

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:52.240
to effortlessly access
Anthos-connected clusters

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.400
running anywhere.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.480
It solves key pain points we
have heard from many customers,

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.900
deploying on-prem, at the
edge, or even on other clouds.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.520
Accessing Kubernetes
clusters can be a challenge.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.510
And they often resort to
using jump hosts or VPNs

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.490
to these clusters.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:12.460
Connect Gateway simplifies
the connectivity challenge

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:16.630
by using the same infrastructure
that powers existing connected

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:21.310
Anthos UIs. The service
authenticates and authorizes

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:25.150
GCP users and forwards their
request to the Connect agent

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.810
running in the cluster, and
ultimately, the cluster's

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.730
Kubernetes API server.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:34.060
As a result, you get a
consistent and secure way

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.400
to use your existing
Kubernetes tooling

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.910
to perform common operations
against any fleet-registered

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.000
Kubernetes cluster.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.030
A key building block for Anthos
infrastructure management

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.760
is our software-defined
networking,

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:52.300
as the capabilities in Anthos
extend the full networking

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.120
stack from layer 2 to layer 7.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.490
And it's built on
best-in-class Kubernetes

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.980
open-source infrastructure.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.810
We have extended
the capabilities

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.410
with innovations such as
node network firewalls

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:08.650
to protect workload at
infrastructure level.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.200
Load balancing for Kubernetes
workload is bundled

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.220
in by taking in solutions
like MetalLB and extending it

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.590
for layer 4 /
layer 7 use cases ,

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.770
as well as integrating
BJP support into it.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:24.050
We also enable
connectivity workloads

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:27.920
across hybrid environments with
easy-to-deploy VPN solution

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:31.340
and provide consistent
observability and affordability

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:32.860
tools.

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:34.750
There are two important
features that we

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:38.650
have introduced in Anthos
networking stack this year.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:43.220
Anthos Dataplane v2 is optimized
for Kubernetes networking.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.400
It is an opinionated
data plane that

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:49.060
leverages Cilium, an
open-source project that

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.420
makes the Linux kernels
Kubernetes-aware using AVP app.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:55.730
It delivers a unified
user experience

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:58.670
for configurations,
deployments, and monitoring

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:02.510
of networking features across
Google Cloud, hybrid, and even

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:04.960
other cloud environments.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.380
It makes parts.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.040
The first cluster is
a networking ecosystem

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.540
with better performance
and visibility.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:15.750
Another key feature is
Anthos networking gateway,

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.880
which provides a number
of advanced networking

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.670
features for hybrid and
multicloud environments.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.100
For example, Egress
NAT enables you

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.640
to have a deterministic
IP for egress traffic

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.910
with namespace and
level granularity.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:31.710
With multi-cluster
connectivity, you

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:35.430
get direct part reachability
between environments.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.700
Well, I hope you're as
excited about these additions

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:38.780
as we are.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.560
And wait, we have a
lot more to talk about.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:44.200
I'll invite Lisa to share
more exciting capabilities

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.570
that we've added to Anthos.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:48.710
LISA SHEN: Thank you, Alok.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:52.190
Now let's take a look at Anthos
Configuration Management.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.580
It enables you to automatically
deploy shared environment

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.910
configurations and enforce
approved security policies

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.750
across Kubernetes clusters
on premises, on GKE,

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.570
and in your other
public cloud platforms.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.090
As part of Anthos
Configuration Management,

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:10.910
we have recently announced
a new feature available

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.970
called Config Controller,
a hosted service

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:17.810
to provision and orchestrate
Google Cloud resources.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:20.870
The service offers
an API endpoint

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:22.740
that can provision
and orchestrate

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.520
more than 120 Google
Cloud resources

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.640
the same way it manages
Kubernetes resources,

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:32.750
with continuous monitoring
and self healing.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:34.730
As a hosted service,
you don't have

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:37.520
to install or manage the
components of the Config

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.420
Controller or be an expert in
Kubernetes resource management,

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:44.820
because Google Cloud
will manage them for you.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.450
Config Controller
provisions infrastructure,

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:50.060
applications, and
cloud services,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.520
configs them to meet
your desired intent,

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:56.060
and monitors them for
the configuration Git.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:58.970
Config changes are as
easy as a Git push,

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.520
and can be easily integrated
with your development

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.180
workflows.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.790
Another exciting new feature
we've introduced this year

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.490
is the ACM Multi-Repo Support.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:13.000
Enabling Multi-Repo mode
lets you sync configurations

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.020
from multiple repositories
to the same set of clusters.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:19.780
An example is shown
in the diagram here.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:23.200
The platform admin manages
the centralized infrastructure

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.960
for the organization
and enforces policies

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:30.390
on the cluster and on all
namespaces in the org.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:33.810
The application developers who
are responsible for managing

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.300
live deployments
apply configurations

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:39.690
to applications in the
namespaces they work on.

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:43.200
The Multi-Repo feature decouples
the config deployment lifecycle

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.760
for different teams.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.030
It provides you with more
autonomy and flexibility.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.060
And you get to choose where
you want to place the repo

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:52.590
and how to structure it.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.260
Microservice architectures
present numerous benefits

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.680
but also introduce challenges
like added complexity

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:05.500
and fragmentation for
different workloads.

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:09.220
Anthos Service Mesh, which is
a Google fully managed service,

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.460
simplifies service
delivery across the board,

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:15.910
from traffic management and
mesh telemetry to security

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.420
and communications between
different services.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:22.410
We have many exciting new
features rolled out in ASM

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:22.950
this year.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.250
And all of these are Anthos
value-add over the Istio

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.080
open source project.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.380
With the managed ASM
control plan on GCP,

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:34.960
Google is responsible for the
availability, scalability,

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:37.200
and the security of
the control plane.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:40.530
We also have managed
certification on GCP

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:44.660
and managed proxy updates
based on the release channels.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:48.080
Both SRE dashboard with
service-level objectives

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:49.970
and the Security
Insights dashboard

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:52.490
are available to give
you in-depth visibility

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.800
into the application services.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.410
With the application
Security Insights dashboard,

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:01.790
you will have a 360-degree
view of workload security.

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.130
You get a summary of the
security configuration

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:06.470
with the workload drilldown
view to troubleshoot

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.300
security issues.

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.330
In addition, you can now
add Google Compute Engine

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:15.050
VMs in managed instance
groups to a service mesh.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:17.930
You'll get the same
observability, telemetry,

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.180
and security
capabilities as you would

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.730
get from running
services on Anthos GKE

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:23.930
clusters in the mesh.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:31.550
Anthos Identity Service is an
authentication proxy for Anthos

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.650
that enables customers
to authenticate

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:35.600
their users to
Anthos environment

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.510
using their existing
identity solutions.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:41.060
AIS simplifies
identity management

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:43.610
across hybrid and
multicloud deployments

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:46.400
by providing a common
authentication abstraction

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:48.980
layer for all the
Anthos environments.

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.740
As a result, you can leverage
your existing identity

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.830
investment with Anthos
using standard identity

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:59.120
protocols such as LDAP or
OIDC, or you can consistently

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.880
apply your policies
across the environments.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:04.130
It simplifies your
cross-environment workload

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:04.910
migration.

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:11.540
Anthos Workload Identity feature
enables Kubernetes workload

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.360
hosted on any cluster
to authenticate

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.120
to Google Cloud or other
services in a consistent way.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.690
We have extended GKE
workload identity

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:25.220
concept to Anthos platforms
on-prem and in multiclouds.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.560
The Workload Identity
Federation feature

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:30.320
eliminates the need for
long-lead credentials

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.260
and replaces them with
short-lead, autorotated

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:34.640
credentials.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.710
It enables consistent
authentication

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.080
across all the clusters.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.600
The feature not only helps
application developers

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:45.060
to connect applications to
essential services easier,

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.940
but also helps
the platform admin

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.310
to maintain the governance
across the clusters.

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:54.140
On the application
management side,

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.780
I'm very excited that we
will have Anthos Hybrid

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.950
CI/CD available for public
preview in November this year.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:03.080
Built on top of the
existing Google Cloud

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.569
Build and the Google
Cloud Deploy solution,

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:09.619
Anthos Hybrid CI/CD will allow
customers to build and deploy

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:12.079
containerized
applications seamlessly

00:17:12.079 --> 00:17:14.839
to hybrid and multicloud
environments using

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:16.920
a single control plane.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:21.869
Hybrid Cloud on-cluster
CI/CD is ideal for customers

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:25.020
that are prohibited from
connecting their source control

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:29.640
or artifact repos to GCP due
to organizational, or network,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.800
or security requirements.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.260
More specifically, this
will allow customers

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.170
to run CI/CD workloads
with their private network

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.110
with simplified network
configurations that don't

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:42.340
rely upon network peering.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:46.230
In addition, the on-cluster
CI/CD also reduces the latency

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.330
for accessing local resources.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:54.550
Another popular tool included
in the Anthos product

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:57.760
is Migrate for Anthos and GKE.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.640
Migrate for Anthos
and GKE intelligently

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.160
extracts, migrates,
and modernizes

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:07.510
applications to run natively
on containers in GKE and Anthos

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:08.740
clusters.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:11.590
It makes it easy and
fast to modernize

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:14.740
traditional applications
away from virtual machines

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:16.960
and into native containers.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:21.070
You can migrate VMs running
on VMware, AWS, or Azure

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:24.490
into containers managed
by Anthos in real time

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:28.450
and capitalize on increased
resource utilization, unified

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.600
logging and monitoring, and
modern application lifecycle

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:33.490
management tools.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:35.560
A recent feature
that we've introduced

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.420
is called the Migrate
Fit Assessment

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:40.540
Tool, which helps customers
discover their inventory

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:43.480
and assess fit for
containerization.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.480
It replaces the existing
Migrate Discovery Tool

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:50.690
and it runs completely
disconnected from the internet.

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:53.440
In addition, Migrate
for Anthos and GKE

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:55.840
now supports enhanced
container runtimes

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.820
such as Anthos Cloud Run.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.010
Cloud Run is a managed
compute platform

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.840
that enables you to run
stateless containers that

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:06.640
are invocable by web
request or Pub/Sub events.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:09.520
The enhancements let you
deploy your migrate container

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:11.190
workloads on Cloud Run.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:17.090
You can learn more about
Anthos via Google Cloud Product

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:20.570
website or explore our powerful
multi-cluster Kubernetes

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:23.390
management features with
the Anthos sample deployment

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:24.380
tutorial.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:27.410
When you are ready, be sure
to schedule your application

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:29.360
assessment workshop
with your Google team

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.910
to begin planning your
application modernization

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:32.630
journey.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:34.130
Thank you for watching the show.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:36.810
And I hope you have a great day.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:37.810
ALOK JAIN: Thanks, Lisa.

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:39.730
And thank you, everyone,
for being with us

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:41.810
on What's New With Anthos.

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:45.660
We hope you enjoy the rest
of the Google Next 2021.

